## Define project risk correctly

There is no historical 0–9 target in the source data. Do not train a ten-class classifier. Train models against an observable historical outcome and then translate the validated model output to a risk index.

### Primary outcome

Use:

`Withdrawal_Within_36_Months = 1`

when a project has a valid withdrawal date between 0 and 36 months after its queue date. Otherwise use 0 only when the project has a complete 36-month observation window.

Make the horizon configurable through `OUTCOME_MONTHS = 36`.

Determine whether a project is old enough to observe the entire outcome window. Do not treat recent active projects as negative examples merely because they have not withdrawn yet. Exclude withdrawn projects with missing withdrawal dates from the fixed-horizon model and report how many were excluded.

### Optional survival-analysis extension

Because many active projects are right-censored, add a survival-analysis section if date quality is sufficient. Use a transparent Cox proportional-hazards or discrete-time survival model. Treat active projects as censored at `DATA_AS_OF_DATE`. Treat completed projects carefully; they leave the risk set at completion when a valid completion date exists. Explicitly report that missing Done Dates prevent a complete competing-risks implementation.

The survival model is a challenger or robustness analysis, not automatically the production choice. Do not select it unless its date assumptions, validation, and interpretation are sound.

